<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
<title>Systems of ODEs and Laplace Transforms</title>

<introduction>

<p>
Work collaboratively with your group to complete the problems at each
station. Show all calculations, justify all classifications, and discuss
your reasoning with your teammates. Use the phase-plane figures whenever
appropriate.
</p>

</introduction>

<exercise workspace="1in">

<title>Modeling with Systems of Differential Equations</title>

<p>
Consider the system
</p>

<md>
\mathbf{x}'
=
\begin{bmatrix}
4 &amp;  -2\\
1 &amp;  1
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}.
</md>

<ol>

<li>
Identify the coefficient matrix.
</li>

<li>
How many dependent variables are present?
</li>

<li>
Determine the trace and determinant.
</li>

<li>
Write the characteristic polynomial.
</li>

<li>
Without solving the system completely, predict whether the equilibrium
will be stable or unstable.
</li>

<li>
Give one real-world application that could be modeled by a system of
differential equations.
</li>

</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">

<title>Real Eigenvalues and Phase Portraits</title>

<p>
Consider the system
</p>

<md>
\mathbf{x}'
=
\begin{bmatrix}
-5 &amp;  2\\
-2 &amp;  -1
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}.
</md>

<ol>

<li>
Find the characteristic polynomial.
</li>

<li>
Determine the eigenvalues.
</li>

<li>
Find an eigenvector corresponding to each eigenvalue.
</li>

<li>
Classify the equilibrium point.
</li>

<li>
Determine whether the origin is stable or unstable.
</li>

<li>
Explain how the eigenvalues predict the long-term behavior of the system.
</li>

</ol>

<p>
Reference Phase Portrait:
</p>
<image width="50%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}

\draw[->] (-3,0)--(3,0);
\draw[->] (0,-3)--(0,3);

\draw[dashed] (-2.5,-2.5)--(2.5,2.5);

\draw[->] (-2,1) .. controls (-1,.3) .. (-.3,.1);
\draw[->] (-2,2.2) .. controls (-.7,.8) .. (-.2,.2);

\draw[->] (2,-1) .. controls (1,-.3) .. (.3,-.1);
\draw[->] (2,-2.2) .. controls (.7,-.8) .. (.2,-.2);

\draw[->] (-2.2,-2.2)--(-1,-1);
\draw[->] (2.2,2.2)--(1,1);

\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
<p>
Which classification from the course notes best matches this figure?
</p>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">

<title>Complex Eigenvalues</title>

<p>
Consider the system
</p>

<md>
\mathbf{x}'
=
\begin{bmatrix}
2 &amp;  -3\\
3 &amp;  2
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}.
</md>

<ol>

<li>
Find the characteristic polynomial.
</li>

<li>
Determine the eigenvalues.
</li>

<li>
Identify
<m>\alpha</m>
and
<m>\beta</m>.
</li>

<li>
Classify the equilibrium point.
</li>

<li>
Is the origin stable or unstable?
</li>

<li>
Will trajectories spiral inward or outward?
</li>

<li>
Write the exponential factor appearing in the solution.
</li>

</ol>

<p>
Reference Phase Portrait:
</p>
<image width="50%">
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.9]

\draw[->] (-3,0)--(3,0);
\draw[->] (0,-3)--(0,3);

\draw[
domain=0:6*pi,
samples=300,
smooth,
->]
plot ({0.08*\x*cos(\x r)},
      {0.08*\x*sin(\x r)});

\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);

\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<p>
Explain how the phase portrait matches the eigenvalues.
</p>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">

<title>Laplace Transform Matching</title>

<p>
Match each function with its Laplace transform.
</p>

<table>

<tabular>

<row>
<cell>Function</cell>
<cell>Transform</cell>
</row>

<row>
<cell><m>1</m></cell>
<cell>____________________</cell>
</row>

<row>
<cell><m>e^{2t}</m></cell>
<cell>____________________</cell>
</row>

<row>
<cell><m>\sin(3t)</m></cell>
<cell>____________________</cell>
</row>

<row>
<cell><m>\cos(2t)</m></cell>
<cell>____________________</cell>
</row>

</tabular>

</table>

<p>
Available transforms:
</p>

<md>
\frac{1}{s},
\qquad
\frac{1}{s-2},
\qquad
\frac{3}{s^2+9},
\qquad
\frac{s}{s^2+4}.
</md>

<ol>

<li>
Which transform pair was easiest to identify?
</li>

<li>
Which transform pair required the most discussion?
</li>

</ol>


</exercise>

<exercise workspace="1in">
<title>Inverse Laplace Transforms</title>

<p>
Determine each inverse Laplace transform.
</p>

<ol>

<li>

<md>
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{5}{s+4}
\right\}
</md>

</li>

<li>

<md>
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{2}{s^2}
\right\}
</md>

</li>

<li>

<md>
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{s}{s^2+9}
\right\}
</md>

</li>

<li>

<md>
\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{1}{s(s+1)}
\right\}
</md>

<p>
Use partial fraction decomposition.
</p>

</li>

</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
Consider the three-dimensional system
</p>

<md>
\mathbf{x}'
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &amp;  1 &amp;  0\\
0 &amp;  1 &amp;  1\\
0 &amp;  0 &amp;  2
\end{bmatrix}
\mathbf{x}.
</md>

<ol>

<li>
Find the eigenvalues.
</li>

<li>
Which eigenvalue dominates the long-term behavior?
</li>

<li>
As
<m>t\to\infty</m>,
does the origin appear stable or unstable?
</li>

<li>
Explain your reasoning.
</li>

</ol>

</exercise>

</handout>
